    "profile_text": {
        "body": "Amazon Web Services is identified in public filings as a public company with ticker AMZN and CIK 0001018724, founded in 1994 and headquartered in Seattle, Washington. Its website for Amazon SageMaker describes the next generation of SageMaker as the center for all data, analytics, and AI. The site says SageMaker Unified Studio brings together AWS machine learning and analytics capabilities in a single development environment, with SageMaker AI features including HyperPod, JumpStart, and MLOps, and Amazon Q Developer, which the site describes as the most capable generative AI assistant for software development. The service is said to support data in data lakes, data warehouses, and third-party or federated sources, with governance built in. The site also mentions Amazon S3 data lakes, Amazon Redshift data warehouses, Apache Iceberg-compatible tools, zero-ETL integrations, and Amazon SageMaker Catalog for permissions, data classification, toxicity detection, guardrails, and responsible AI policies. It includes statements from a customer in automotive operations and from Carrier about unifying data and accelerating lakehouse implementation.\n\nThe company's AI products include Amazon SageMaker and AWS Bedrock. Amazon SageMaker is listed with full ML lifecycle management, model monitoring, bias detection, and explainability tools. AWS Bedrock is listed as a managed foundation model API service with IAM integration, VPC support, model evaluation tools, and AgentCore for agent orchestration. The SageMaker site positions the service as secure by design, spanning the AI lifecycle from integrated development environments and distributed training to inference, AI ops, governance, and observability. It also mentions Amazon Athena, Amazon EMR, and AWS Glue for analyzing, preparing, and integrating data using open source frameworks.\n\nPublic filings report revenue of $716,924,000,000 for the year ended December 31, 2025, on a 10-K. Washington state law exposure records list 114 entries for Amazon Web Services. Court records list Amazon Web Services in Haugland v. Anthropic PBC in the District of Delaware.",
